From: pseudo <aws@pseudoseed.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 03:41:21 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 28/35] [Spec 250][Phase: phase_10] feat: approve a gate from
 t3code, over a same-origin proxy
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The gate is approved from t3code's web app over codev-agent's existing
capability path, and the panes finally read the phase and messages
codev-agent has been publishing since phase 6.

The proxy's upstream is SERVER-configured (T3CODE_CODEV_AGENT_ORIGINS) and
the browser selects among the entries by id. A proxy that forwards to an
origin the browser names is an SSRF primitive, and a route-path allowlist
does not constrain the host.

t3code's own session gates USE of the proxy and is never forwarded:
`authorization` and `cookie` are absent from the forward set, which is an
allowlist rather than a denylist because this hop sees credentials. The
Connection header's own named tokens are subtracted from that allowlist
too, since that header names headers that are themselves hop-by-hop.

Unreachable and silent keep separate signals, and a redirect from the
configured origin is refused rather than passed on — handed to the page,
the browser would follow it cross-origin.

The approval record comes from the server. Four outcomes survive the port:
approved, refused, unconfirmed and sessionEnded.
